I guess it's time for another go-round regarding the protection 
racket--if only to prevent its promoters from giving out all the advice 
to newbies.

It was inscribed by Kent Paul Dolan in the annals dated 2/2/05 9:25 PM:
> "Martin Read" <mpread@chiark.greenend.org.uk> wrote:
> 
>>I would strongly dispute the validity of the
>>protection racket for wombats; the protection
>>racket requires a great deal of attention to
>>detail that goes against what seems to be the
>>point of playing a wombat, which is that the
>>wombats are very straightforward to play.
> 
> 
> That's bogus.
> 
> Combat wombats are "straightforward" merely because
> spellcasting can be long ignored in a successful
> game. They are still subject to precisely the same
> game mechanics and considerations as other
> characters: buff pets are good, killing stuff
> prevents your pet from getting buff by your pet
> killing it instead of you killing it; protection is
> by far cheapest bought when the PC is at XL:1;
> higher XL causes tougher monsters, and so on.

"And so on" would include: pets that kill everything usually eat 
everything, frequently causing hunger problems in the player character; 
avoiding levelling up prevents *you* from getting buff, leading to easy 
hp deaths should you get separated from your pet or attacked while your 
pet "eats noisily"; monster difficulty still increases as you go deeper 
in dungeon level, although more slowly than if you levelled up.
>  
>>Intrinsic AC 1 costs 2800-4000 gold, on average,
>>for a first-level character.
> 
> Exactly why one should attempt the protection
> racket, it costs large multiples of that if not,
> deferring protection acquisition to much later in
> the game, hurting survival odds a different way,
> but not less than, the protection racket run-up
> does.
> 
By what method do you compare how survival odds are hurt by deferring 
protection to later in the game?
> 
> If your pet dies to mischance, the protection racket
> is a non-starter, but this is still backwards
> thinking. Your pet's survival potential goes up as
> it gets buff, and it gets buff only if you let it
> fight. The more monsters you defer to your pet, the
> faster your pet grows strong, the fewer rooms it has
> to explore while wimpy, and the better its survival
> chances are in traps when it does encounter them.

Yes, but it also has a tendency to engage in combat a lot, causing its 
hp often to be far below max. Moreover, *you* as a character playing the 
racket are depending heavily on your pet. If it dies halfway to Minetown 
in the Mines, you're in a poor position to advance *or* retreat, leading 
to easy death.
> 
>>Also, be careful with your pet when you get to
>>minetown; it might just decide it fancies some
>>tasty watch captain meat, and the watch captain
>>has a *much* better AC than it does.
> 
> A problem whether protection racketing or not is
> that the game code for a pet's decision that
> attacking a monster is appropriate behavior is
> braindead, leading to too much "into the valley of
> death rode the large cat" activity. 

The game is realistic in this aspect. Dogs chase cars and challenge 
other animals far beyond their ability to defeat them.

> This isn't an
> argument pro or con for the protection racket. 

Sure it is. If you're depending on your psycho braindead cat for 
survival, you're looking for trouble.

> once you've reached Mines Town and the priest is at
> hand, losing the pet isn't the issue it was. If it
> happens, finish buying protection, retreat to
> Dlvl:1, and there become buff "yourself" while
> hoping for a new kitten, puppy, or pony to train.

Retreating from Minetown to dlvl 1 is easier said than done in that 
circumstance.

